.\" ai-tools-records(5) -- the record stream a report of Agent Tools Restricted writes for a machine consumer.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst and the RPM
.\" %prep), like ai-tools(1). The columns, the escape and the identity recipe stated here are the
.\" ones records-base.lib.sh and records-tsv.lib.sh implement; tests/unit/records.sh holds the
.\" COLUMNS section to the library's registry and runs its REFERENCE DECODER over the library's fixtures.
.TH AI-TOOLS-RECORDS 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools-records \- the record stream a report writes for a machine consumer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
observed-at occurred-at code record-id severity finding subject-type operator item subject detail
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A report that runs unattended writes what it found as a
.B record stream
on standard output:
a header row, then one row per finding, each ending in a line feed,
every row holding the same number of tab-separated fields as the header.
.B "ai-tools-admin system post-upgrade --check"
and
.B "ai-tools projects claim --format tsv"
write one.
The exit status states whether the reading was complete
(see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ),
so a cron job or a monitoring backend reads the stream and the status together.
.PP
The header is printed only before the first row, so a clean run prints
nothing and exits 0.
One invocation is one stream:
concatenating the output of several runs into one file loses where each
run begins and ends, and
.B observed-at
does not restore that.
.PP
Every field is written in one byte escape
(see
.BR "FIELD ENCODING" ),
so a path holding a tab, a line feed or a byte that is not UTF-8 arrives
exactly as it is on disk.
An empty field means
.IR "not applicable, or not available" .
.SH COLUMNS
The header names the columns in this order.
A consumer selects a column by name, not by position.
.SS Fixed columns
.TP
.B observed-at
The collection time,
.BR YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ ,
in UTC.
One value per run.
.TP
.B occurred-at
The event time, in the same form, where the row is an event; empty otherwise.
.TP
.B code
The message code of the situation, as
.BR ai-tools-messages (7)
lists it.
.TP
.B record-id
The identity of the situation on its subject
(see
.BR "RECORD IDENTITY" ).
.SS Enumerated columns
.TP
.B severity
One of
.BR ok ,
.BR info ,
.B attention
and
.BR unreadable .
.TP
.B finding
The report's own token for the situation
.RB ( hook-missing ,
.BR copy-kept ,
.BR error ).
.TP
.B subject-type
One of
.BR file ,
.BR directory ,
.BR unit ,
.BR agent ,
.BR integration ,
.BR project ,
.B operator
and
.BR host .
Taken from the subject, not from the finding.
.SS Identifier columns
.TP
.B operator
The account whose project or registry the row concerns; empty for a
host-wide row.
.TP
.B item
The thing inside the subject the row is about: a hook, a key, a rule.
A list of components, framed as
.B ITEMS
states.
.SS Path columns
.TP
.B subject
The path, unit, agent or operator the row is about.
.SS Text columns
.TP
.B detail
Display prose for a person.
It is not part of the contract: a reader does not parse it, and its wording
changes between releases.
.SH SEVERITY AND EXIT STATUS
The producer folds every row's severity into its exit status:
5 when any row is
.BR unreadable ,
else 4 when any row is
.BR attention ,
else 0.
.B info
and
.B ok
do not change it.
A reader that meets a severity token it does not know treats the row as
.BR attention ,
and a known
.B unreadable
in the same stream still wins.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The reading was complete and found no finding of severity
.B attention
or
.BR unreadable .
.TP
.B 1
An operation the command was asked to perform failed.
On a command that changes the host this wins over every other status.
.TP
.B 4
The reading was complete and left at least one finding of severity
.BR attention .
.TP
.B 5
The reading could not be made in full: a source the report needs failed,
a line from an upstream tool could not be parsed, or a row could not be
formed.
The rows printed before that point are still valid observations.
.PP
On a command that changes the host the order is 1, then 5, then 4:
a requested change that failed is 1; otherwise a final assessment that could
not be read is 5; otherwise findings left over are 4.
.SH COMPLETION
The exit status states whether the reading was complete:
.TP
.B a required source failed
A collector exited non-zero, a file could not be read, a helper is missing.
The report writes its
.B error
finding with severity
.B unreadable
where it still can, and exits 5.
.TP
.B an upstream line could not be parsed
An
.B error
row, severity
.BR unreadable ,
with
.B detail
naming the tool; exit 5.
.TP
.B a scan reached its cap
A
.B scan-capped
row, severity
.BR attention ,
with
.B item
holding the cap; exit 4.
The rows collected before the cap stay valid.
.TP
.B an optional capability is not installed
An
.B info
row where the report prints those, or none; the exit is unchanged.
.TP
.B a source outside the requested scope
No row and no change to the exit, and the same for a path the scan
excludes by design.
.PP
A consumer resolves an earlier finding from its absence only when the run
completed (exit 0 or 4) without a
.B scan-capped
row, over the same host, operator, report and scope.
A path a scan excludes is outside that scope, so its absence does not resolve
anything either.
A run with exit 5, a signal, a malformed row, or a truncated final line is
incomplete.
.SH FIELD ENCODING
The producer writes every field in one canonical escape, and the output is
free of NUL:
.TP
.B a byte 0x20 to 0x7e other than \e
is written as itself.
.TP
.BR \e ", tab, line feed and carriage return"
are written
.BR \e\e ,
.BR \et ,
.B \en
and
.BR \er .
.TP
.B every other byte
is written
.B \ex
and two lowercase hex digits.
.PP
A reader:
.IP \(bu 3
accepts empty standard output with exit 0 as the clean result:
the one stream that has no header;
.IP \(bu 3
splits a row on the literal tab byte and strips only the terminating line
feed.
It does not strip whitespace and does not split on runs of whitespace,
so leading, adjacent and trailing empty fields keep their positions.
A shell reader does not use
.B "IFS=$'\et' read"
for that reason: tab is IFS whitespace, and the shell collapses adjacent empty
fields;
.IP \(bu 3
rejects the row on a raw byte outside 0x20 to 0x7e,
an unknown or truncated escape,
a
.B \ex
escape for a byte that has a literal or named form
(0x20 to 0x7e, 0x09, 0x0a, 0x0d),
.BR \ex00 ,
uppercase hex digits,
and a field count that differs from the header's;
.IP \(bu 3
rejects a non-empty stream whose first row is not a header, and a header
with a duplicate column name or a missing canonical column;
.IP \(bu 3
keeps a column it does not know, and passes through a code, a finding token
or a subject type it does not know.
.PP
.B REFERENCE DECODER
applies these rules in Python.
.SH ITEMS
.B item
is composed while collecting, never parsed out of
.BR detail .
It is a list of components, framed so the list is recoverable from the field.
The producer refuses an empty component.
The list with no components is written as the empty field.
A non-empty list is framed in three steps:
.IP 1. 4
each component is encoded on its own, as
.B FIELD ENCODING
states, so it does not hold a raw tab;
.IP 2. 4
the encoded components are joined with one raw tab;
.IP 3. 4
the joined value is the field's value, encoded again when the row is
written, like every field.
.PP
A reader reads the empty field as the empty list.
Otherwise it decodes the field, splits the result on the raw tab, decodes each
part, and rejects the row when a part is empty.
The framing applies to a single component too, so a one-component item and a
two-component item cannot produce the same field.
With
.B TAB
standing for the raw byte:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
components          after step 2      field on the wire
["a TAB b", "c"]    a\etb TAB c        a\e\etb\etc
["a", "b TAB c"]    a TAB b\etc        a\etb\e\etc
["key"]             key               key
.EE
.RE
.PP
For a component of printable ASCII without
.B \e
\(em every key, hook event and token \(em the framing leaves the value as it
was.
.SH RECORD IDENTITY
.B record-id
is the first 16 lowercase hex digits of SHA-256 over exactly these bytes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ENC(code) 0x09 ENC(subject) 0x09 ENC(item)
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B ENC
is the escape of
.BR "FIELD ENCODING" ,
and
.B item
is the framed value of
.BR ITEMS .
There is no header and no trailing line feed, and no other field is hashed.
An encoded value does not hold a raw tab, so the three parts are recoverable
from the hashed bytes.
The id is stable across changes to
.BR detail ,
.B severity
and the timestamps, and changes when the situation, the subject or any item
component changes.
.PP
The id names a situation on a subject within one host.
It is not unique across hosts, is not a resource URI, an authorization token
or an idempotency key, and repeated polls produce the same id.
A consumer that stores records keys them on host, report,
.BR operator ,
.B subject-type
and the identity tuple, and uses
.B record-id
for correlation within that key.
For a lifecycle view of one subject across findings (not fixed, then fixed),
a consumer joins on that key with
.B code
dropped.
.PP
Known answers, with
.B TAB
for the raw byte and every other byte as written:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
code      subject                item              record-id
MSG-A1B2  /srv/project/app.conf  (empty)           d2b6a0f255f321d9 \" ref-index: ignore
MSG-A1B2  /srv/project/app.conf  ["key"]           6bc5171e35197c4f \" ref-index: ignore
MSG-A1B2  /srv/project/a TAB b   ["hooks", "x"]    142b7f86fe379965 \" ref-index: ignore
.EE
.RE
.PP
The third hashes the bytes
.B "MSG-A1B2 TAB /srv/project/a\etb TAB hooks\etx" \" ref-index: ignore
where
.B \et
is the two-character escape.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
A column is appended after
.BR detail .
Renaming, reordering or removing a column is a breaking change, and so is a
change to a field's meaning, a code's meaning, a finding token's meaning, the
item framing or the identity recipe.
A new code, finding token or subject type may appear in any release.
.PP
The tab-separated form does not distinguish an empty value from an absent
one.
A later JSON form carries the same fields and computes
.B record-id
the same way, preserves a byte that is not valid UTF-8 through an explicitly
encoded companion value rather than replacing it, and keeps
.B item
as its component list.
.SH A BACKEND CONSUMER
A backend runs the command through its documented command and privilege
boundary, never by sourcing shell libraries.
It runs the command
.B without a controlling terminal
(a systemd unit, a cron job, or
.BR setsid (1)),
because a command that can ask a question asks on
.I /dev/tty
whenever one exists, whatever its standard streams are redirected to.
It passes every choice it has authority for as an option.
It captures standard output, standard error and the exit status separately,
waits for the process to end before accepting a report, and keeps the exact
bytes and the invocation context.
It shows report health in its response data.
It does not turn exit 4 into a transport failure, and does not retry a
command that changes the host because its report was incomplete.
.SH REFERENCE DECODER
This decoder applies every rule of
.B FIELD ENCODING
and
.BR ITEMS .
.B decode_field
returns the bytes one field encodes,
.B decode_item
returns an item's components,
.B read_stream
returns one stream's rows as dictionaries of bytes, and
.B exit_status
folds their severities as the producer does.
Each raises
.B ValueError
on input the rules reject.
.PP
.\" records-decoder-begin
.RS 4
.EX
import re

ESCAPE = re.compile(rb'\e\e(\e\e|t|n|r|x([0-9a-f]{2}))')
NAMED = {b'\e\e': b'\e\e', b't': b'\et', b'n': b'\en', b'r': b'\er'}
RAW = re.compile(rb'[^\ex20-\ex7e]')
CANONICAL = ('observed-at', 'occurred-at', 'code', 'record-id',
             'severity', 'finding', 'subject-type', 'operator',
             'item', 'subject', 'detail')


def decode_field(field):
    """Return the bytes a field encodes, or raise ValueError."""
    if RAW.search(field):
        raise ValueError('raw byte outside 0x20-0x7e')
    out = bytearray()
    i = 0
    while i < len(field):
        if field[i:i + 1] != b'\e\e':
            out += field[i:i + 1]
            i += 1
            continue
        m = ESCAPE.match(field, i)
        if m is None:
            raise ValueError('unknown or truncated escape')
        if m.group(2) is None:
            out += NAMED[m.group(1)]
        else:
            b = int(m.group(2), 16)
            if b == 0 or 0x20 <= b <= 0x7e or b in (0x09, 0x0a, 0x0d):
                raise ValueError('escape for a byte with a shorter form')
            out.append(b)
        i = m.end()
    return bytes(out)


def decode_item(field):
    """Return the item's components, or raise ValueError."""
    if field == b'':
        return []
    parts = [decode_field(p) for p in decode_field(field).split(b'\et')]
    if any(p == b'' for p in parts):
        raise ValueError('empty item component')
    return parts


def read_stream(data):
    """Return one stream's rows as dicts of bytes, or raise ValueError."""
    if data == b'':
        return []
    if not data.endswith(b'\en'):
        raise ValueError('truncated final line')
    lines = data[:-1].split(b'\en')
    cells = lines[0].split(b'\et')
    if any(RAW.search(c) for c in cells):
        raise ValueError('no header')
    header = [c.decode('ascii') for c in cells]
    if len(set(header)) != len(header):
        raise ValueError('duplicate column')
    if any(c not in header for c in CANONICAL):
        raise ValueError('missing canonical column')
    rows = []
    for line in lines[1:]:
        fields = line.split(b'\et')
        if len(fields) != len(header):
            raise ValueError('field count differs from the header')
        rows.append(dict(zip(header, [decode_field(f) for f in fields])))
    return rows


def exit_status(rows):
    """Return the exit a producer reports for these rows."""
    status = 0
    for row in rows:
        severity = row['severity']
        if severity == b'unreadable':
            return 5
        if severity not in (b'ok', b'info'):
            status = 4
    return status
.EE
.RE
.\" records-decoder-end
.SH EXAMPLES
Read a report from a cron job and keep the stream only when the reading
found something:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ai-tools-admin system post-upgrade --check > /var/tmp/post-upgrade.tsv
case $? in
    0) rm -f /var/tmp/post-upgrade.tsv ;;
    4) logger -t post-upgrade "findings left; see the stream" ;;
    5) logger -t post-upgrade "reading incomplete; see the stream" ;;
    *) logger -t post-upgrade "the command failed" ;;
esac
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai-tools (1),
.BR ai-tools-admin (8),
